when we think of the American Civil War  we often think of several things of its  leaders like Abraham Lincoln and  Jefferson Davis of Confederate and Union  troops shooting it out in bloody combat  or of what the Civil War was all about  what isn't often considered is the role  that Native Americans played in the  conflict and how the Civil War affected  them so what did Native Americans  actually do who did they side with and  what did they think of the American  Civil War it shouldn't surprise you that  Native Americans weren't exactly keen on  the white Americans who had  progressively been taking more and more  land from them over the previous  centuries the primary tribes that were  affected by this were collectively known  as the Five Civilized tribes who had  previously been forced westward in the  decades prior to the outbreak of the  Civil War these five tribes were the  Cherokee the creeks the Seminole with  the Chickasaw and the Choctaw and all of  them sided with the Confederacy largely  because they have the Washington  government it's responsible for their  forced deportations and also because  many of them had economies dependent on  slaves not in their entirety of course  many within these tribes had sympathies  towards the Union with respect to  abolitionism not only that but one major  concern was the repercussions for  choosing the wrong side they'd already  lost a lot they didn't wish to lose more  these divisions ripped many tribes apart  and led to people from the same family  fighting for both sides one man of  particular importance was a Creek chief  for pottery Yolo who argued that Native  Americans should get involved with the  Civil War because it was the white man's  problem in response to this the  Confederate aligned tribes attacked in  late 1861 and apatheia Hollow fled north  into Kansas where he would remain until  his death in 1863 in early 1862 the  Union launched an offensive into Indian  lands and despite the best efforts of  the Confederate aligned Native Americans  there under the command of Stand Watty  the Confederacy was beaten back soon  after this the Union captured the chief  of the Cherokee and by the end of the  next year it was in control of all of  Native American territory did this mean  that the war was over for Native  Americans no in fact many were sent  eastward to go and fight there whilst  those that remained waged a guerrilla  war the primary leader of the Native  American Raiders was against and Watty  and he spent over a year raiding Union  supply trains what he was so good at his  job that when the Confederates  restructured the army he was placed in  command of all of his Native American  units and promoted to the rank of  Brigadier General and despite the best  efforts of what he in the Native  Americans in the West the Union still  won the war and two months after General  Lee surrendered the Native American  tribes capitulated as well so after the  war what  happened to the Native American tribes  had sided with the Confederacy well  nothing good  whose slaves owned by natives were to be  freed and given full citizenship within  the tribes and what's morally it was a  good thing economically it was  absolutely devastating for these tribes  the tribes were also forced to sell much  of their lands to the federal government  at a reduced rate the money raised from  which was then promptly appropriated as  war reparations of course there were  more than five tribes involved in the  civil war but none of them came out of  it well even those who weren't to  involve like the Comanches or the  Cheyenne's were forced to sign similar  documents which confiscated their lands  and moved them to reservations the  federal government soon headed by  veterans of the Civil War who for the  most part had a distaste for their  former Native American enemies ignored  these treaties where it suited them  tribes would lose yet more land to be  placed on reservations and would spend  over a decade trying to fix the  divisions which had played them during  the Civil War I hope you enjoyed this  episode and thank you for watching with  the special thanks to my patreon  supporters who you can see on screen  here and with an extra special thanks to  James Bissonnette David archaeologist  party-boy Co Danny Maloney Colin  Castleman Rob Waterhouse are in the  white Michael Reynolds H way an emperor  chris wicker Gustav Swann Gareth Turner  spinning three plates David Silverman  Winston Cawood Maggie packs Kowski  Christian check' Anthony Beckett  Robert Wetzel Skye Chappell and Ike
 

